<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>An 83-year-old man with three weeks of visible blood in the urine with small clots, a weak stream, and chronic retention (post-void residual 460 mL) with acute kidney injury, now drained by an indwelling catheter. Please evaluate the hematuria with cystoscopy, advise on the retention and the voiding trial, and interpret his PSA. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Soon, within one to two weeks; the bladder is drained and he is stable. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What is the cause of the bleeding? Please perform cystoscopy and review the CT urography.</item><item>If a bladder tumor is found, what resection and staging do you advise, and what treatment options would suit a frail man of 83?</item><item>How should the retention be managed: how long should the catheter stay, and when and how should a trial without catheter be done?</item><item>How should his PSA of 6.4 ng/mL be interpreted, and is prostate MRI or biopsy warranted at his age?</item><item>Is it safe to manage the catheter and any clots at home, and what should prompt urgent return?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Voltaire is seen at the request of Dr. Delacroix-Mensah, one week after a catheter was placed for chronic retention with gross hematuria. The urine in the bag has cleared from red to light pink, with one small clot four days ago. He has had no fever, flank pain, or catheter blockage. He describes several years of a slowing stream and getting up at night, worse over the past few months, and three weeks of visible blood. He has stopped the diphenhydramine and the phosphate enemas. He has never smoked, has no exposure to aromatic amines, has not had pelvic radiation or cyclophosphamide, has not travelled where schistosomiasis is common, and has no history of bladder stones. He takes no anticoagulant or antiplatelet drug. He is frank that he would not want major surgery if it meant months of recovery. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Voltaire, the pen name of François-Marie Arouet, was born in Paris; his birth certificate gives 21 November 1694, although he stated on several occasions that he was in fact born on 20 February. He was born into a wealthy bourgeois family; his putative father, François Arouet, became a receiver in the Cour des Comptes (the audit office), and he lost his mother when he was seven. He adopted the name Voltaire in 1718. [D]</item><item>He was imprisoned in the Bastille for nearly a year in 1717 after mocking the regent, and in May 1726, after a quarrel with the chevalier de Rohan, was beaten, taken to the Bastille, and conducted to Calais, whence he left for London. [D]</item><item>In a letter to the baron de Breteuil dated December 1723, he gave an account of the smallpox from which he was recovering: he fell ill on 4 November at Maisons; the disease declared itself after two days of fever with a slight eruption; the physician Gervasi had him take an emetic eight times and drink two hundred pints of lemonade, he had himself bled a second time on his own authority, and by the 15th he was out of danger. [D]</item><item>His biographer Jean Orieux, as summarized in a 1970 review in the Revue d&apos;histoire de la pharmacie, describes a lifelong preoccupation with illness and remedies: he suffered from colic, took as many as eight purges and twelve enemas in a month, was persuaded he would die before forty, and really did suffer from his kidneys and bladder. In a letter of 1769 he wrote that being very ill was the common lot of old age, especially for one who had always had a feeble constitution. [D]</item><item>He returned to Paris on 10 February 1778, for the first time in 28 years, to direct rehearsals of his tragedy Irène; more than 300 people called on him the day after his arrival. On 30 March he went to the Académie amid acclamations and was crowned in his box at a performance of Irène. His deteriorating health did not permit his return to Ferney. [D]</item><item>His secretary Jean-Louis Wagnière recorded that on 25 February 1778, at a quarter past noon, while dictating from his bed, he coughed three times and said that he was spitting blood, and that blood then spurted from his mouth and nose. A declaration in his hand of 2 March 1778 states that he had been attacked for four days by a vomiting of blood, and the blood loss continued for several days. [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

