    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Visible blood in the urine for three weeks with clots, and a weak stream, straining, and frequent small voids, in a man of 83; under evaluation</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R31.0</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem echoing his documented urinary complaints and last illness; superseded by the diagnosis at the third chronic-care visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Chronic urinary retention with overflow (post-void residual 460 mL at intake), worsened by a nightly anticholinergic sleep aid; drained by an indwelling catheter</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R33.8</td><td>S — Synthetic finding echoing the strangury and catheterization documented in his last weeks.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Acute kidney injury from bladder outlet obstruction (creatinine 1.68 mg/dL, from 1.1 mg/dL a year earlier)</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>N17.9</td><td>S — Synthetic finding; it echoes, without asserting, the later encyclopedia term uremia.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Benign prostatic hyperplasia with lower urinary tract symptoms: prostate about 78 mL on CT, enlarged, firm, and smooth on examination, PSA 6.4 ng/mL with a PSA density of 0.08; no prostate biopsy, by shared decision</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>N40.1</td><td>S — Synthetic diagnosis echoing the very large prostate described at the 1778 autopsy; whether that prostate was malignant is unknown (CLM-0012).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Chronic constipation, lifelong by his account, with long use of laxatives and self-given enemas</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>K59.09</td><td>S — Synthetic modern problem echoing his documented colic, purges, and enemas; no diagnosis of inflammatory bowel disease is made.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

