<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Gross hematuria with difficult urination (R31.0). The bleeding comes from a solid bladder tumor whose appearance and size, with wall thickening and perivesical stranding on CT, suggest a muscle-invasive cancer; the keratinizing plaques and squamous atypia on cytology raise the possibility of squamous histology. The friable prostatic vessels may add to the bleeding. The upper tracts are clear and the chest CT shows no metastasis. [S]</item><item>Chronic urinary retention (R33.8). Chronic high-pressure retention from prostatic obstruction, made worse by the anticholinergic sleep aid and possibly by clot and the tumor&apos;s bulk; kidney function has recovered with drainage. [S]</item><item>Benign prostatic hyperplasia with lower urinary tract symptoms (N40.1). PSA 6.4 ng/mL with a gland of about 78 mL gives a PSA density of 0.08, low, and the gland is smooth. Retention, catheterization, and bleeding all raise the PSA. At 83, with a bladder tumor that will determine his treatment and outlook, finding a prostate cancer now would not change management, so I do not advise prostate MRI or biopsy. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: CT urography two days after intake: 4.2 cm sessile soft-tissue mass of the posterior bladder wall extending to the dome, wall thickened to 1.8 cm with perivesical stranding; no hydronephrosis; upper tracts clear; prostate about 78 mL with a median lobe; no enlarged nodes or bone lesions. Chest CT: clear. Urine culture: no growth. Urine cytology: atypical squamous cells suspicious for malignancy. Creatinine 1.68 mg/dL at intake and 1.24 mg/dL today (eGFR 58); hemoglobin 11.6 then 11.2 g/dL; PSA 6.4 ng/mL; IPSS 22 before the catheter; post-void residual 460 mL. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Transurethral resection of the bladder tumor with examination under anesthesia in two days, resecting into muscle for diagnosis and staging; one dose of perioperative antibiotic. [S]</item><item>Keep the indwelling catheter until after the resection; then a trial without catheter on tamsulosin once he is free of infection, with a bladder scan after voiding; if he fails, intermittent self-catheterization or a long-term catheter rather than prostate surgery while the bladder cancer is treated. [S]</item><item>Continue tamsulosin; no 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor for now. [S]</item><item>No prostate MRI or biopsy, by shared decision; no further PSA testing unless it would change care. [S]</item><item>If the resection confirms muscle-invasive cancer, complete staging is already in hand with the CT urography and chest CT; a geriatric assessment should precede the treatment decision, and the options to discuss are radical cystectomy, bladder-preserving radiotherapy, weekly palliative radiotherapy, and symptom care, weighed against his goals. [S]</item><item>At home: catheter care, 1.5 to 2 liters of fluid a day, and urgent return or a call for a blocked catheter, large clots, fever, or rigors. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Delacroix-Mensah the same day. Voltaire agrees to the resection and asked that the pathology be explained to him plainly at his next primary care visit. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Resection in two days. I will review the pathology with Dr. Delacroix-Mensah and see him again if bleeding, clots, or retention return, or if he wishes to reconsider surgery. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
